perf(pip): Turn the diagnostic probes off by default, and close the two that escaped the switch

The probes found every fault in this chain - the exposure, the projection, the withheld lens - but
they are not free, and the chain is settled enough to stop paying for them in normal play.

What each one costs:
 - every svp_probe_rt reads a full render target back to the CPU: a staging copy of a 1920x1200
   surface plus a blocking Map, i.e. a stall of a few milliseconds each time, and they ran four at a
   time every 240 frames;
 - the per pass line (pass view=...) was written every pass of every frame, which is what made the
   log grow by megabytes per session;
 - the BMP dumps write every pixel of full sized surfaces to disk (tens of megabytes each).

r__svp_debug now defaults to 0, which removes all of it: no readbacks, no dumps, no per frame log.
For a diagnostic session "r__svp_debug 1" restores the throttled readbacks and the per pass lines
(now also throttled to every 120 frames so a debug session does not tank the frame rate), and
"r__svp_debug 2" adds the images.

Two probes had escaped the switch and are now behind it as well: the albedo probe in phase_combine
(a readback, every 600 frames) and the stable overlay line in the DLSS integration (a Msg, every
300). The image dumps are level 2 only now instead of also running rarely at level 1, which is what
their own comment had always claimed.

What remains in normal play is cheap and worth keeping: the per optic line (edge triggered, on optic
changes) and the state line (one line every 300 frames, only while an optic is magnifying).